\DOC LOCAL_MUL_SAFETY_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MUL_SAFETY_TAC : string -> int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Discharges a subroutine call in an {eventually} symbolic-execution goal by
applying a named assumed specification theorem, advancing the goal's state
variable past the call and replacing the pre-call state facts with post-call
ones.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument {assump_name : string} is the label of a universally
quantified {ensures} specification occurring among the goal's assumptions; it
justifies the call. The second argument {n : int} is the index of the current
symbolic state, fixing the state variable used in the conclusion and in the new
assumptions.

Applied to a goal {A ?- eventually ... s_n} where {A} contains the labelled
specification together with facts about state {s_n} (register and memory reads,
flag values, the event trace, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation from the
initial state):
{
   A ?- eventually ... s_n
   =========================================  LOCAL_MUL_SAFETY_TAC assump_name n
   A' ?- eventually ... s_(n+1)
}
\noindent Here {A'} drops the pre-call facts about {s_n} and instead contains
corresponding facts about {s_(n+1)}: a new program-counter equation, an
extended {MAYCHANGE} relation, the preserved register/memory reads, and a new
event-trace equation prefixing a freshly introduced trace variable {z}, plus
two further assumptions characterising {z} — its defining equation and an
in-bounds memory-access property. The labelled specification assumption and the
unrelated assumptions of {A} are kept, with the labelled one moved to the end.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces the behaviour is this single-goal state advance, on
goals whose assumptions include both a labelled specification and a full set of
facts about the current symbolic state.

\ENDDOC
